<header><p class="st-kicker">other · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Dominion of Ceylon</h1><p class="st-meta">1948 CE – 1972 CE · capital Colombo</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Sri Lanka's first independent state, a Commonwealth dominion that won freedom peacefully and later became a republic.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/british-ceylon">British Ceylon</a> gained independence on 4 February 1948 under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceylon-independence-act-1947">Ceylon Independence Act</a>, with the British monarch remaining head of state and a Governor-General. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/d-s-senanayake">D. S. Senanayake</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-national-party">United National Party</a> became the first prime minister, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soulbury-constitution">Soulbury Constitution</a> provided a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westminster-system">Westminster-style</a> parliament.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The new state disenfranchised most plantation <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamils">Tamils</a> of Indian origin through the citizenship laws of 1948 and 1949. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/s-w-r-d-bandaranaike">S. W. R. D. Bandaranaike</a> won the 1956 election and passed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sinhala-only-act">Sinhala Only Act</a>, deepening tension with Tamils. After his assassination in 1959, his widow <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sirimavo-bandaranaike">Sirimavo Bandaranaike</a> became the world's first woman prime minister in 1960. A youth insurrection by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janatha-vimukthi-peramuna">JVP</a> was suppressed in 1971.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>A new constitution adopted on 22 May 1972 ended dominion status and renamed the country <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-gopallawa">William Gopallawa</a>, the last Governor-General, becoming its first president and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sirimavo-bandaranaike">Sirimavo Bandaranaike</a> remaining prime minister.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/dominion-of-ceylon.jpg" alt="Dominion of Ceylon" width="320" height="177"><figcaption>Image: Zscout370 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Ceylon_(1948%E2%80%931951).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1948 CE – 1972 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Colombo</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>D. S. Senanayake, S. W. R. D. Bandaranaike, Sirimavo Bandaranaike</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Theravada Buddhism, Hinduism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Sinhala, Tamil, English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Ceylon (dominion)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1960&amp;focus=dominion-of-ceylon" aria-label="Dominion of Ceylon on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6891 1919 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Dominion of Ceylon lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7211 2175L7211 2175M7211 2175L7212 2176"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7211 2175h0M7212 2176h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7211 2175h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7211 2175h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Colombo. <a href="/atlas?year=1960&amp;focus=dominion-of-ceylon">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
